define_power_model	CommandSpec { name: "define_power_model", pack_source_sandbox_helper: false, traits: Traits(0), surface: Some([SpecSurface { provider: Core(Tcl), windows: [("8.4", Some("9.2"))] }]), arity: Arity { count: Arguments, min: 2, max: 65535, step: 0, also_exact: None }, arity_windows: [], arg_roles: [], arg_role_resolver: Some(fn), arg_role_count_resolver: None, arg_role_layout_resolver: None, arg_role_resolver_roles: [Body], clause_grammar: None, arg_presentation: [], repeated_args: [], frame_effect: None, clause_shape_check: None, command_prefixes: [], command_prefix_resolver: None, script_timing_resolver: None, script_lookup_scope: None, substitution_resolver: None, callback_taint_inputs: [], return_type: None, return_type_hook: None, var_write_typing: ReturnValue, variable_write_min_args: None, return_elements: None, var_elements_effect: None, representation_effect: None, arg_types: [], subcommands: [], prefix_matching: Enabled, allow_unknown_subcommands: false, default_form_first_word: None, hover: Some(HoverSnippet { summary: "Define a power model.", synopsis: ["define_power_model power_model_name ?-for model_list? ?-implementation? ?-update? ?-complete? {UPF_commands}"], snippet: "Creates a named, reusable block of UPF commands describing the power intent of a model, executed later when apply_power_model runs it, in a protected environment isolated from outer and global Tcl variables in both directions like a Tcl proc body. Depending on the macro attributes set by apply_power_model on the target instance, the same definition describes already-implemented intent, yet-to-be-implemented intent, a plain encapsulation of UPF merged with the parent context, or a component power model for system power-consumption modeling. A power model can be parameterized with add_parameter and updated incrementally with -update until finalized with -complete, and cannot be nested inside another power model definition.", source: "IEEE 1801-2024 §6.31", examples: "defi