tifies a set of sequential elements within a power domain that require retention behaviour and specifies the save and restore control signals or conditions, the retention supply, and the corruption and behavioural parameters. The targeted set is computed from -elements minus -exclude_elements, either of which may reference instances, named processes, state elements, signal names or a named retention element list (see 6.54), and is filtered by -applies_to; precedence resolution (5.7) picks the applicable strategy when more than one could apply to the same element. The save and restore signals apply to balloon-style retention registers, while live-primary and live-secondary implementations specify retention behaviour through -retention_condition instead (see 6.53.1).", source: "IEEE 1801-2024 §6.53", examples: "set_retention retCpu  -domain           PDCPU  -retention_supply PDCPU.ret  -save_signal      {lRet negedge}  -restore_signal   {lRet posedge}  -elements         cpuRetList", return_value: "The empty string if successful; otherwise a TCL_ERROR is raised." }), forms: [FormSpec { kind: Default, synopsis: "set_retention retention_name -domain domain_name ?-elements element_list? ?-exclude_elements exclude_list? ?-applies_to flop|latch|any? ?-retention_supply ret_supply_set? ?-no_retention? ?-save_signal {logic_net high|low|posedge|negedge} -restore_signal {logic_net high|low|posedge|negedge}? ?-save_event_condition {boolean_expression}? ?-restore_event_condition {boolean_expression}? ?-powerdown_period_condition {boolean_expression}? ?-restore_period_condition {boolean_expression}? ?-async_set_reset_effect ignored|retained_value|output_value? ?-use_retention_as_primary? ?-parameters {RET_SUP_COR|NO_RET_SUP_COR|SAV_RES_COR|NO_SAV_RES_COR...}? ?-instance {{instance_name ?signal_name?}...}? ?-update? ?-retention_power_net net_name? ?-retention_ground_net net_name? ?-save_condition {boolean_expression}? ?-restore_condition {boolean_expression}? ?-retention_condition {